    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. it sounds like it just loses its grip. but sometimes the noise is really intermittent and hard to pin down. i've heard people say things like 'there's a gremlin in the engine' when they can't figure out why a car is acting up. is there actually a mechanical reason why an issue would feel so random or unpredictable like that?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, the unpredictability of a squealing belt often stems from conditional factors like small amounts of loose coolant spiking the surface temperature or dust and debris temporarily reducing friction. These intermittent events happen because the belt's performance depends heavily on consistent tension (given by an adjusted tensioner) and compound thermal conditions. Eventually, significant wear or damage leads to consistent failure, but the remaining few weeks of \"warning signs\" can appear sporadic and frustrating.",
